<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Godfrey of Bouillon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–<a href="/years/1100">1100</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Godfrey of Bouillon was Duke of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, son of Count Eustace II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>. He pawned his castle of Bouillon to finance the expedition. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099 he became its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ruler, taking the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, and died the next year, succeeded by his brother Baldwin I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon.jpg" alt="Godfrey of Bouillon" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_of_Bouillon_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Godfrey was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eustace-ii-count-of-boulogne">Eustace II, Count of Boulogne</a>. His uncle, Godfrey the Hunchback, was murdered on 22 February 1076 without issue, having designated him as heir, and Godfrey became Lord of Bouillon through an inheritance from his mother's family. His claim was challenged by several rivals, among them his aunt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a>, Albert III of Namur and Count Theoderic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a>. Emperor Henry IV first gave the duchy of Lower Lorraine to his own infant son Conrad. Godfrey supported Henry during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> and gained Lower Lorraine in 1087.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1096 Godfrey joined the First Crusade with his brothers Eustace and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a>. He either sold or mortgaged most of his estates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> to raise an army, reportedly 40,000 strong, which set out in August 1096. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in November and, like most of the crusaders, agreed to a modified oath to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> promising to restore some lands. He took part at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> and the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><p>The crusaders reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in June 1099, and the major attack took place on 14 and 15 July. Godfrey and some of his knights were among the first to take the walls. On 22 July a council met in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond IV of Toulouse</a> refused the crown, Godfrey agreed to rule, preferring the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre</a> to that of king. Its meaning is disputed, since early chroniclers used the term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> or his old rank of duke, while later ones wrote rex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Godfrey defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a> in August 1099 and in 1100 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and Caesarea tributaries. Godfrey died on 18 July 1100. The Arab chronicler Ibn al-Qalanisi reports that an arrow killed him at Acre, but Christian chroniclers say he fell ill at Caesarea, and a disease like typhoid is thought more likely than poison. His supporters seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and his brother Baldwin succeeded him; Dagobert crowned Baldwin as first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> king on 25 December 1100.</p><p>Later accounts idealised him as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>'s military leader and as a legislator of the assizes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and in the early 14th century he was chosen as one of the Nine Worthies. In reality he was one of several leaders, and the assizes developed gradually. He was the hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a>'s epic Jerusalem Delivered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Godfrey of Bouillon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The leaders chose <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b>, duke of Lower Lorraine, to govern the city; he declined the title of king and was known as Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors gathered further counties and rights, and in 1096 Bishop Otbert bought the castle and lordship of Bouillon from <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b>, who needed money for the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the crusaders stormed Jerusalem in July 1099, their leaders chose <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b> to govern the city; he declined the title of king and was known as Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>At a council in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, after Raymond of Toulouse had refused the crown, <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b> was elected to rule Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bohemond, Raymond IV of Toulouse and <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b> took positions at different gates for a siege of nine months.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Eustace II fought for William the Conqueror at Hastings in 1066, and his sons <b>Godfrey of Bouillon</b> and Baldwin I led the First Crusade and ruled Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.73,1.61&amp;year=1086" aria-label="Boulogne-sur-Mer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4587 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Godfrey of Bouillon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5037 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5037 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.73,1.61&amp;year=1086">What was here in 1086? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
